Local fixes for SDL 3.4.14 (147a8ee32dbf9ac02f3794964490687b6bbda1bc, the
port's third_party/SDL), Nintendo 3DS audio driver (src/audio/n3ds), built
with devkitARM r68 and libctru 2.7.0.

1. N3DSAUDIO_WaitDevice waited on its condition with no time limit. The
   condition is signalled only when the DSP hands a wave buffer back
   (AudioFrameFinished, from ndsp's thread), on a DSP cancel, and in
   N3DSAUDIO_CloseDevice, which SDL calls only after it has joined the audio
   thread (SDL_audio.c, ClosePhysicalAudioDevice). A buffer the DSP never hands
   back -- one queued across a sleep that the DSP did not resume -- left the
   audio thread waiting for ever, and SDL_CloseAudioDevice waiting for the
   audio thread. The wait now wakes every 50 ms and looks at the shutdown flag
   and the buffer again; while the DSP keeps playing nothing changes.
2. N3DSAUDIO_CloseDevice freed the wave buffers right after ndspExit, which is
   safe only when ndspExit really stops ndsp (it then joins ndsp's thread and
   stops the DSP). When the program holds its own ndsp reference as well, as
   the 3DS port does (3ds/i3DS.cpp), ndspExit only drops SDL's reference, and
   the DSP may still be reading the buffers for the frame it is on. Wait for
   two ndsp frames first (one to send the channel reset, one for the DSP to
   act on it), at most about 20 ms.

Apply with: git -C <SDL clone> apply <this file> (tools/3ds/build_sdl.sh does)

diff --git a/src/audio/n3ds/SDL_n3dsaudio.c b/src/audio/n3ds/SDL_n3dsaudio.c
index ded98ea..d72c8f5 100644
--- a/src/audio/n3ds/SDL_n3dsaudio.c
+++ b/src/audio/n3ds/SDL_n3dsaudio.c
@@ -200,12 +200,20 @@ static bool N3DSAUDIO_PlayDevice(SDL_AudioDevice *device, const Uint8 *buffer, i
     return true;
 }
 
+// The condition is only signalled when the DSP hands a buffer back
+// (AudioFrameFinished), on a cancel, or by CloseDevice, which SDL calls only
+// after it has joined this thread. A buffer the DSP never hands back (one
+// queued across a sleep that the DSP did not resume) would leave this thread
+// here for ever, and SDL_CloseAudioDevice waiting for it. So the wait wakes
+// now and then to look at the shutdown flag (and the buffer) again.
+#define N3DSAUDIO_WAIT_SLICE_NS (50LL * 1000000LL)
+
 static bool N3DSAUDIO_WaitDevice(SDL_AudioDevice *device)
 {
     contextLock(device);
     while (!device->hidden->isCancelled && !SDL_GetAtomicInt(&device->shutdown) &&
            device->hidden->waveBuf[device->hidden->nextbuf].status != NDSP_WBUF_FREE) {
-        CondVar_Wait(&device->hidden->cv, &device->hidden->lock);
+        CondVar_WaitTimeout(&device->hidden->cv, &device->hidden->lock, N3DSAUDIO_WAIT_SLICE_NS);
     }
     contextUnlock(device);
     return true;
@@ -235,6 +243,18 @@ static void N3DSAUDIO_CloseDevice(SDL_AudioDevice *device)
 
     contextUnlock(device);
 
+    // When something else also holds ndsp, ndspExit below neither stops the
+    // DSP nor joins ndsp's thread, so the DSP may still read the wave buffers
+    // for the frame it is on. Give ndsp's thread two frames (one to send the
+    // channel reset, one for the DSP to act on it) before they are freed: at
+    // most about 20 ms, as the frame count stands still while the DSP sleeps.
+    {
+        const u32 frame = ndspGetFrameCount();
+        for (int i = 0; i < 10 && (u32)(ndspGetFrameCount() - frame) < 2; i++) {
+            svcSleepThread(2LL * 1000000LL);
+        }
+    }
+
     ndspExit();
 
     if (device->hidden->waveBuf[0].data_vaddr) {
